// 2+2W in the release-acquire fragment -- the SRA-vs-RA separating behaviour.
//
// rmm-zoo edge: SRA strictly weaker than RA is refuted by this; it is the
//               witness for the RA-vs-SRA ordering edge.
// Source: Lahav, Giannarakis & Vafeiadis, "Taming Release-Acquire Consistency"
//         (POPL 2016); Lahav & Boker, "What's Decidable about Causally
//         Consistent Shared Memory?" (TOPLAS 2022), Ex. 3.6.
//         Vendored shape: rmm-zoo litmus/strictly-weaker/SRA-vs-RA/2+2W.
//
// Each thread writes both locations and then reads the OTHER thread's write to
// the location it wrote second. Reading r0 = r1 = 1 forces mo to run against
// program order on both locations, closing a cycle in (hb u mo):
//   W(x,1) -po-> W(y,2) -mo-> W(y,1) -po-> W(x,2) -mo-> W(x,1).
// SRA's strong-write-coherence forbids that cycle. RA's write-coherence only
// forbids mo;hb, and neither mo step is followed by hb back to its source, so
// RA ALLOWS it.
//
// Reference verdicts: RA and WRA ALLOW. SRA and SC FORBID.
x := 0;
y := 0;
{
  x.store(1, rel);
  y.store(2, rel);
  r0 := y.load(acq)
} ||| {
  y.store(1, rel);
  x.store(2, rel);
  r1 := x.load(acq)
}
%%
allow (r0 = 1 && r1 = 1) [RA]
